</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float">Listing 2: </span>Example of shared bugs in operation-similar APIs within the DL framework.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM for Fuzzing.</span>
As LLMs continue to evolve, researchers are using them for fuzz testing DL frameworks
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, for tasks such as seed generation, test case mutation, and constraint extraction.
LLMs trained on vast amounts of text data have demonstrated powerful performance in natural language processing tasks.
Additionally, some variants of LLMs (such as CodeLlama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, CodeGemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, and Qwen2.5-Coder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>) are also trained and fine-tuned on specific code snippet datasets, enabling them to handle code-related tasks (such as program analysis, code generation, etc.).
This allows LLMs to generate code snippets that satisfy the complex syntax and semantic requirements of DL framework APIs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem Statement</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">New Threat.</span>
DL framework APIs enable users to interact with the framework for tasks like dataset processing, model building, training, evaluation, and inference.
Interestingly, APIs from different frameworks are often developed independently but share similar functionality and design.
We observe significant commonalities across different DL framework APIs, driven by three main factors.
First, many DL frameworks share essential operations such as tensor computation, automatic differentiation, and dataset handling.
Second, the underlying logic of supported algorithms is consistent, such as the mathematical principles behind convolution and pooling in convolutional neural networks (CNNs<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>).
Finally, the enhancement of interoperability between frameworks has driven API consistency. For example, most frameworks now support the ONNX standard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> to facilitate model import, export, and conversion.
Due to code reuse and compatibility, there are also many similar APIs within the framework itself.
Inspired by the well-known hypothesis that similar systems may exhibit similar bugs, we speculate that APIs within and across DL frameworks may share bugs, and we investigate this through empirical study.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motivation Eaxmple.</span>

We collect API bug reports from GitHub Issues for four popular DL frameworks, including TensorFlow<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, PyTorch<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, OneFlow<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, and Jittor<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>.
We observe that a shared bug refers to a defect occurring in similar APIs within or across frameworks. These defects are often caused by common designs, logic, or implementation patterns, resulting in similar issues.
Moreover, we analyze the occurrence of these shared bugs as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operation Similarity APIs.</span>
We find that APIs with similar operations often share similar bugs, both within and across frameworks.
For example, as shown in Listing <a href="#LST1" title="Listing 1 ‣ II-A Fuzzing DL Framework ‣ II Background &amp; Problem Statement ‣ MirrorFuzz: Leveraging LLM and Shared Bugs for Deep Learning Framework APIs Fuzzing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torch.nn.AdaptiveMaxPool2d</span> and <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">flow.nn.AdaptiveAvgPool1d</span> triggered the same crash due to an excessively large <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">output_size</span> parameter.
While one performs max pooling and the other average pooling, both are pooling operations for downsampling feature maps.
Therefore, we categorize this type of API as <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">operation-similar</span> APIs.
A pair of APIs is classified as <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">operation-similar</span> if they perform similar functions and produce comparable outputs when provided with analogous inputs.
Similarly, Listing <a href="#LST2" title="Listing 2 ‣ II-A Fuzzing DL Framework ‣ II Background &amp; Problem Statement ‣ MirrorFuzz: Leveraging LLM and Shared Bugs for Deep Learning Framework APIs Fuzzing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">operation-similar</span> APIs within the same framework can also share bugs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter Similarity APIs.</span>
We observe that APIs within or across frameworks may share certain parameters, even though their functionality or operations differ significantly.
Interestingly, these shared parameters often result in similar bugs.
For example, both <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torch.nn.Conv2d</span> and <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torch.nn.functional.avg_pool2d</span> crash when the <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stride</span> is set to zero.
Despite performing different operations (padding and pooling), the crash is triggered by the same parameter with the same underlying cause.
In PyTorch version 1.2, the bug in <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torch.nn.Conv2d</span> gets fixed, and calling it with a zero stride raises a runtime error stating that “non-positive stride is not supported”.
However, in version 1.5, <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torch.nn.functional.avg_pool2d</span> still crashes with a zero stride.
We further observe that even in the latest versions, APIs with stride parameters, such as <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torch.ao.nn.quantized.Conv1d</span>, still crashes due to the same issue.
This highlights two points: 1) APIs with different functionalities but similar parameters may also share bugs. 2) fixing a bug in one API does not always address similar issues in others with the same parameter, as each API may implement its functionality independently.
Moreover, as shown in Listing <a href="#LST4" title="Listing 4 ‣ II-B Problem Statement ‣ II Background &amp; Problem Statement ‣ MirrorFuzz: Leveraging LLM and Shared Bugs for Deep Learning Framework APIs Fuzzing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, this case also shares bugs between frameworks.
We classify such pairs of APIs as <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">parameter-similar</span> APIs, which share comparable input parameters, even though they may perform different operations or tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">parameter-similar</span> APIs serve as an important complement to <span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">operation-similar</span> APIs, and we provide definitions for both types of APIs in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Similar APIs Matching ‣ III Approach ‣ MirrorFuzz: Leveraging LLM and Shared Bugs for Deep Learning Framework APIs Fuzzing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a>, along with the details of matching these two types of APIs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="LST3" class="ltx_float ltx_lstlisting">
<div id="LST3.2" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_lstlisting ltx_listing"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64," download="">⬇</a></div>
